{"id":31924374967650,"type":"song","name":"Yakety Yak","artist":"The Coasters","year":1958,"weight":14,"info":{"cover":"https://cdn-images.dzcdn.net/images/cover/8a4598fe4c6bac6a3a740ed1788307a7/250x250-000000-80-0-0.jpg","album":"Yakety Yak","yt":"epCN0f7FTIY"},"artist_ref":{"id":243657217553935,"name":"The Coasters","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The_Coasters_Publicity_Photo.jpg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[],"lists":[{"id":132335824845498,"name":"Billboard Year-End Hot 100","src":"charts","grp":"chart","list_year":null,"rank":21,"year":1958,"note":null,"total":6841,"scaruffi":null},{"id":158051727660304,"name":"Billboard Year-End 1958","src":"singles","grp":"billboard_year_end","list_year":1958,"rank":21,"year":1958,"note":"","total":51,"scaruffi":null}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"This song featured a new style for the Coasters, with a unison lead vocal by Carl Gardner and Billy Guy, harmonized by Cornel Gunter, rather than the previous alternating single lead vocalist. The chorus had the group singing \"Yakati Yak,\" with Dub Jones taking the single line \"don't talk back.\" The formula of unison vocals and King Curtis's saxophone part became a staple for the group. Max Stoller was convinced it would be a massive success, but Jerry Lieber initially wanted a proven, safe song for the B-side. The B-side featured a version of the old song \"Zing went the strings of my heart,\" performed as a duet by Dub Jones and Cornel Gunter. \"Yakati Yak was, of course, a number one hit single.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 68: \"Yakety Yak\" by the Coasters","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dtMHDD0-oOo"}]}],"book_tracks":[{"id":31924374967650,"artist":"Coasters","artist_id":243657217553935,"title":"Yakety Yak","year":1958,"ess":0,"yt":"epCN0f7FTIY","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Sc